<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Province of Westphalia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Prussian Province of Westphalia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> created in 1815 from former prince-bishoprics, counties and principalities of the Westphalian region, with its capital at Münster. It absorbed many small territories, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minden">Minden</a> and Rietberg. In 1946 it was merged into the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia.jpg" alt="Province of Westphalia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Preu%C3%9Fen_-_Provinz_Westfalen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
